Body temperature at the trial
Body temperature was recorded at the sprint and bite trials, but not at the clinging trials.


Show code
temp_long &lt;- bind_rows(
  analysis |&gt; transmute(species, sex, trait = "Sprint speed", tb = tb_sprint, perf = sprint),
  analysis |&gt; transmute(species, sex, trait = "Bite force", tb = tb_bite, perf = bite)
) |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(tb), !is.na(perf)) |&gt;
  group_by(trait, species, sex) |&gt;
  mutate(z = (log(perf) - mean(log(perf))) / sd(log(perf))) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  mutate(trait = factor(trait, c("Sprint speed", "Bite force")))

ggplot(temp_long, aes(tb, z, colour = species)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "#d9d8d4", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_point(
    position = position_jitter(width = 0.04, height = 0, seed = 1),
    size = 1.4, alpha = 0.55, stroke = 0
  ) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, se = FALSE, linewidth = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~trait, scales = "free_x") +
  scale_colour_species() +
  labs(x = "Body temperature at trial (°C)", y = "Standardised log performance (SD)") +
  theme_lizard() +
  guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 2.5, alpha = 1, linewidth = 0)))








Figure 3: Log performance, standardised within species × sex to remove mean differences, against body temperature at the trial. Lines are linear fits per species. Points are jittered ±0.04 °C because temperatures were recorded to 0.1 °C.







Show code
temp_slopes &lt;- temp_long |&gt;
  group_by(trait) |&gt;
  summarise(slope = coef(lm(z ~ tb))[2])


Within species and sex, sprint speed increases with temperature (pooled slope 0.15 SD per °C). Bite force increases clearly only in A. carolinensis. Temperature therefore enters the model as a predictor of sprint and bite. Over this narrow range of temperatures a linear effect is adequate.
